// Define a class for managing data collection from network streams
class DataCollector {
  constructor(realTimeDatabase) {
    this.realTimeDatabase = realTimeDatabase;
    this.dataArray = [];
    this.isPaused = false;
    this.stream = null;
  }

  // Method to start collecting data from the stream
  async start(streamUrl) {
    try {
      this.stream = await fetch(streamUrl);
      const reader = this.stream.body.getReader();
      while (!this.isPaused && !this.stream.signal.aborted) {
        const { done, value } = await reader.read();
        if (done) break;
        this.dataArray.push(value);
        // Simulate real-time database write operation
        await this.realTimeDatabase.write(value);
      }
    } catch (error) {
      console.error('Error collecting data:', error);
    } finally {
      this.stream?.cancel();
    }
  }

  // Method to pause the data collection process
  pause() {
    this.isPaused = true;
    console.log('Data collection paused');
  }

  // Method to resume the data collection process
  resume() {
    this.isPaused = false;
    console.log('Data collection resumed');
  }

  // Getter for the collected data
  getData() {
    return this.dataArray;
  }
}

// Define a class for interacting with a real-time database
class RealTimeDatabase {
  constructor() {
    this.databaseRef = null;
  }

  // Method to initialize the real-time database reference
  init(ref) {
    this.databaseRef = ref;
  }

  // Asynchronous method to write data to the database
  async write(data) {
    if (this.databaseRef) {
      try {
        await firebase.firestore().collection('data').add(data);
        console.log('Data written successfully');
      } catch (error) {
        console.error('Error writing data:', error);
      }
    }
  }
}

// Example usage
const realTimeDB = new RealTimeDatabase();
realTimeDB.init(firebase.database().ref());

const collector = new DataCollector(realTimeDB);
collector.start('https://example.com/stream');

// Simulate user interaction
setTimeout(() => {
  collector.pause();
}, 5000);

setTimeout(() => {
  collector.resume();
}, 10000);
```

This code provides a basic structure for the `DataCollector` and `RealTimeDatabase` classes, which manage data collection from network streams and real-time database interactions, respectively. The `start` method collects data from the stream using a fetch API reader and writes it to the real-time database asynchronously. Error handling is included to manage network interruptions and prevent data loss during pauses.

The code also demonstrates how to pause and resume the data collection process using the `pause` and `resume` methods. Additionally, the code provides a simple example of user interaction with the collector object.

Remember to replace `'https://example.com/stream'` with the actual URL of your network stream and initialize Firebase for real-time database interactions before running this code.

