```javascript
// Define the API routes using Express.js
const express = require('express');
const router = express.Router();
const db = require('./db'); // Assume a database connection is established

// Function to update an existing resource using HTTP PUT method
router.put('/resource/:id', async (req, res) => {
  try {
    const resourceId = req.params.id;
    const updatedData = req.body;

    // Retrieve the current resource from the database
    const currentResource = await db.getResource(resourceId);
    if (!currentResource) {
      return res.status(404).json({ error: 'Resource not found' });
    }

    // Perform optimistic concurrency control using versioning (assuming a version field)
    if (updatedData.version !== currentResource.version) {
      return res.status(409).json({ error: 'Stale data detected. Please refresh and try again.' });
    }

    // Update the resource in the database
    const updatedResource = await db.updateResource(resourceId, updatedData);

    // Return the updated resource to the client
    res.json(updatedResource);
  } catch (error) {
    console.error(error);
    res.status(500).json({ error: 'Internal server error' });
  }
});

// Function to delete a resource using HTTP DELETE method
router.delete('/resource/:id', async (req, res) => {
  try {
    const resourceId = req.params.id;

    // Retrieve the resource from the database
    const resource = await db.getResource(resourceId);
    if (!resource) {
      return res.status(404).json({ error: 'Resource not found' });
    }

    // Validate user permissions (assuming an auth service is in place)
    if (!req.user.hasPermission('delete_resource')) {
      return res.status(403).json({ error: 'Unauthorized access' });
    }

    // Delete the resource from the database
    await db.deleteResource(resourceId);

    // Return a new resource with default values to the client
    const defaultValues = { /* Define default values for the resource */ };
    res.json(defaultValues);
  } catch (error) {
    console.error(error);
    res.status(500).json({ error: 'Internal server error' });
  }
});

module.exports = router;
```

This code snippet provides a basic implementation of the required functionality using Express.js and assumes that a database connection (`db`) is established. The `router.put` function handles updating an existing resource, including optimistic concurrency control and error handling for various scenarios. The `router.delete` function handles deleting a resource, validating user permissions, and returning a new resource with default values.

To ensure high concurrency and efficient transaction management, you should use appropriate database transactions and indexing strategies. Additionally, to integrate seamlessly with an external authentication service, you can use middleware or libraries such as Passport.js to handle user authentication and authorization.

The code is modular and well-documented, with clear separation of concerns between different components. You can further enhance this solution by adding more features, such as rate limiting, caching, and analytics, to improve performance and provide a better user experience.

