```javascript
class StreamingDataCollector {
  constructor(apiUrl) {
    this.apiUrl = apiUrl;
    this.paused = false;
    this.dataQueue = [];
    this.worker = null;
    this.listeners = [];
  }

  // Start data collection
  start() {
    if (this.worker) return; // Already running
    this.paused = false;
    this.fetchData();
  }

  // Pause data collection
  pause() {
    this.paused = true;
  }

  // Resume data collection
  resume() {
    this.paused = false;
  }

  // Add a listener for real-time feedback
  addListener(listener) {
    this.listeners.push(listener);
  }

  // Internal method to fetch data from API and process it
  fetchData() {
    if (this.paused) return; // Check pause state

    fetch(this.apiUrl)
      .then(response => response.json())
      .then(data => {
        this.processData(data);
        setTimeout(() => this.fetchData(), 1000); // Fetch data every second
      })
      .catch(error => {
        console.error('Error fetching data:', error);
        setTimeout(() => this.fetchData(), 5000); // Retry after 5 seconds on error
      });
  }

  // Process the received data
  processData(data) {
    if (!this.isValidData(data)) {
      console.warn('Invalid data received');
      return;
    }
    this.dataQueue.push(data);
    this.notifyListeners();
  }

  // Validate incoming data
  isValidData(data) {
    // Add your validation logic here
    return data && data.length > 0;
  }

  // Notify all listeners about new data
  notifyListeners() {
    while (this.dataQueue.length > 0 && !this.paused) {
      const newData = this.dataQueue.shift();
      this.listeners.forEach(listener => listener(newData));
    }
  }

  // Stop the collector and clean up
  stop() {
    if (!this.worker) return; // Not running
    this.paused = true;
    this.worker.terminate();
  }
}
```

This code snippet provides a basic structure for a streaming data collector in JavaScript. It includes methods to start, pause, resume data collection, add listeners for real-time feedback, and stop the collector. The `fetchData` method is responsible for fetching data from an external API, processing it using the `processData` method, and handling errors with retry logic. Data validation is performed by the `isValidData` method, which you can customize according to your needs.

The collector also supports concurrent operation by managing a queue of received data and notifying listeners in real-time as new data becomes available. This ensures that the user interface remains responsive even when large volumes of data are being processed.

To use this collector, simply create an instance and call the `start` method. You can add listeners using the `addListener` method to receive updates about new data. The collector will automatically pause if necessary and resume based on your application's requirements.

